# Covers: a built-in global name may be SHADOWED by a declaration but not
#         REBOUND.  `var Object = 42` adds a slot on the realm's globals
#         and the name reads 42 from then on; `Object = 42` asks to change
#         the binding the built-ins hold and raises TypeError.
# matrix-row: globals-constness
#
# The two are different requests, not two spellings of one.  A bare
# `x = v` resolves the name through the prototype chain before writing —
# that is what makes a typo in an assignment a LookupError rather than a
# silent new global — and the same walk is where it sees that the slot it
# found is constant.  A declaration resolves nothing: it creates a slot on
# the receiver, which shadows whatever the chain held, exactly as
# `obj.x = v` shadows a prototype's `x`.
#
# `class Pair { ... }` is a declaration too, which is why
# objects/class-multi-slot.chk may name a class after the built-in `Pair`.
#
# The rebind is checked FIRST: the declaration on the line after it
# shadows the name for the rest of the session.

var e1 = "no"; try { Pair = 1 } catch (var e) { e1 = e.isA(TypeError) }; e1
Pair == Pair
var Pair = 42; Pair
class Tuple { var a = 7 }; Tuple.a
[00000001] true
[00000002] true
[00000003] 42
[00000004] 7
